1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,397
Someone runs delete from orders,

2
00:00:02,397 --> 00:00:03,642
with no where clause.

3
00:00:03,642 --> 00:00:04,828
Every order is gone.

4
00:00:04,828 --> 00:00:06,665
How fast can you get them back?

5
00:00:07,215 --> 00:00:08,181
If the answer is,

6
00:00:08,181 --> 00:00:09,090
we have backups,

7
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:12,442
the next question matters more: have you ever restored one?

8
00:00:12,992 --> 00:00:15,828
Today you back up a real post gress database,

9
00:00:15,828 --> 00:00:17,088
break it on purpose,

10
00:00:17,088 --> 00:00:17,782
restore it,

11
00:00:17,782 --> 00:00:19,861
and prove every number came back.

12
00:00:20,411 --> 00:00:22,929
A backup you never restored is only a hope.

13
00:00:24,479 --> 00:00:27,551
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

14
00:00:28,101 --> 00:00:32,517
One: the words. Backup, dump, restore, validate, and recovery point.

15
00:00:33,067 --> 00:00:38,443
Two: a live demo on real post gress. We measure the data first, then take a backup.

16
00:00:38,993 --> 00:00:42,662
Three: we break the database with a delete that has no where clause.

17
00:00:43,212 --> 00:00:45,524
Four: we restore into a new database,

18
00:00:45,524 --> 00:00:46,524
and validate it.

19
00:00:46,524 --> 00:00:47,461
Then your turn,

20
00:00:47,461 --> 00:00:50,210
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

21
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:53,512
This is lesson five of the databases domain.

22
00:00:54,062 --> 00:00:56,579
A backup you never restored is only a hope.

23
00:00:58,629 --> 00:01:00,677
Six ideas for backups that work.

24
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:03,216
A backup is a copy of the data,

25
00:01:03,216 --> 00:01:04,500
kept somewhere else.

26
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:06,618
On the same disk is not a backup.

27
00:01:06,618 --> 00:01:08,992
It is a second copy of the same risk.

28
00:01:09,542 --> 00:01:13,702
P G dump makes a logical backup: the S Q L and the rows,

29
00:01:13,702 --> 00:01:14,593
in one file.

30
00:01:14,593 --> 00:01:16,970
The custom format is compressed,

31
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:19,718
and P G restore can pick parts of it.

32
00:01:20,268 --> 00:01:21,855
A restore is the reverse.

33
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:24,014
Restore into a new database first,

34
00:01:24,014 --> 00:01:25,982
never straight over production.

35
00:01:25,982 --> 00:01:28,140
You get to look before you commit.

36
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:29,266
Validate.

37
00:01:29,266 --> 00:01:33,170
Compare numbers you measured before the disaster: row counts,

38
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:33,810
and a sum.

39
00:01:33,810 --> 00:01:34,706
If they match,

40
00:01:34,706 --> 00:01:35,922
the backup is real.

41
00:01:36,472 --> 00:01:39,616
The recovery point is how much data you can lose,

42
00:01:39,616 --> 00:01:41,605
the time since the last backup.

43
00:01:41,605 --> 00:01:44,684
The recovery time is how long the restore takes.

44
00:01:44,684 --> 00:01:45,326
Know both.

45
00:01:45,876 --> 00:01:46,620
Practice it.

46
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:50,467
Teams that restore every month are calm on the day it matters.

47
00:01:50,467 --> 00:01:53,321
Teams that never tested find out the hard way.

48
00:01:54,871 --> 00:01:59,628
Go to the lab folder.

49
00:02:01,178 --> 00:02:03,077
Look around. Type: L S.

50
00:02:03,627 --> 00:02:07,510
A backups folder, still empty, the server data, and the task file.

51
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:18,022
Tell the tools where the server is.

52
00:02:18,572 --> 00:02:25,335
Now P G dump, P G restore, create D B, and P S Q L all find this server by themselves.

53
00:02:25,885 --> 00:02:39,517
Measure the data.

54
00:02:40,067 --> 00:02:40,679
Six orders,

55
00:02:40,679 --> 00:02:43,458
and fourteen thousand four hundred cents in total.

56
00:02:43,458 --> 00:02:45,571
These two numbers are our proof later.

57
00:02:46,121 --> 00:02:54,804
Write the answers.

58
00:02:55,354 --> 00:03:02,308
Take the backup.

59
00:03:02,858 --> 00:03:08,021
Dash capital F C is the custom format, compressed. No output means success.

60
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:12,219
Is the file there?

61
00:03:12,769 --> 00:03:18,060
A few kilobytes. Small, because our database is small. A real one can be gigabytes.

62
00:03:18,610 --> 00:03:29,298
Is it readable?

63
00:03:29,848 --> 00:03:31,308
The table of contents,

64
00:03:31,308 --> 00:03:32,901
filtered for table data.

65
00:03:32,901 --> 00:03:35,025
Customers and orders are inside,

66
00:03:35,025 --> 00:03:36,086
with their rows.

67
00:03:36,086 --> 00:03:37,613
The backup is readable.

68
00:03:38,163 --> 00:03:46,355
The accident.

69
00:03:46,905 --> 00:03:47,677
Delete six.

70
00:03:47,677 --> 00:03:48,661
Six rows gone.

71
00:03:48,661 --> 00:03:49,784
No where clause,

72
00:03:49,784 --> 00:03:50,557
no warning.

73
00:03:50,557 --> 00:03:53,646
This is the mistake we are insuring against.

74
00:03:54,196 --> 00:04:02,708
Count again.

75
00:04:03,258 --> 00:04:05,882
Zero. Production just lost every order.

76
00:04:06,432 --> 00:04:14,283
Create a new database.

77
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:23,025
Restore into it.

78
00:04:23,575 --> 00:04:26,071
No output again. Quiet means it worked.

79
00:04:26,621 --> 00:04:42,152
Validate.

80
00:04:42,702 --> 00:04:43,397
Six orders,

81
00:04:43,397 --> 00:04:45,737
fourteen thousand four hundred cents.

82
00:04:45,737 --> 00:04:48,582
Exactly the numbers from before the disaster.

83
00:04:48,582 --> 00:04:49,910
The backup is proven.

84
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:53,262
Only now would we copy the data back into production.

85
00:04:53,812 --> 00:04:56,180
Run the judge.

86
00:04:56,730 --> 00:05:02,255
Five checks. Measured, backed up, disaster, restored, and validated. Five out of five.

87
00:05:02,805 --> 00:05:04,768
Clean up.

88
00:05:05,318 --> 00:05:07,984
The server is stopped and everything is removed.

89
00:05:08,534 --> 00:05:16,748
Is it gone?

90
00:05:17,298 --> 00:05:19,815
No response. Nothing is left behind.

91
00:05:21,365 --> 00:05:26,336
Your task. Back up the shop database, survive a disaster, and prove the restore.

92
00:05:26,886 --> 00:05:28,364
One: measure the orders.

93
00:05:28,364 --> 00:05:31,568
Write the count and the total into answers dot text,

94
00:05:31,568 --> 00:05:32,369
one per line,

95
00:05:32,369 --> 00:05:34,032
before you change anything.

96
00:05:34,582 --> 00:05:36,760
Two: take a backup with P G dump,

97
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:38,344
into the backups folder,

98
00:05:38,344 --> 00:05:40,918
and check that P G restore can read it.

99
00:05:41,468 --> 00:05:44,348
Three: run the accidental delete, and count again.

100
00:05:44,898 --> 00:05:46,754
Four: create a new database,

101
00:05:46,754 --> 00:05:48,079
and restore into it.

102
00:05:48,079 --> 00:05:49,868
Five: validate the numbers,

103
00:05:49,868 --> 00:05:50,861
then lab check,

104
00:05:50,861 --> 00:05:51,789
then lab down.

105
00:05:53,839 --> 00:05:57,615
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

106
00:05:58,165 --> 00:05:59,813
The lab lives on GitHub,

107
00:05:59,813 --> 00:06:00,980
at it dash waves,

108
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:02,560
slash dev ops dash A I,

109
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:04,070
in the folder academy,

110
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:04,757
slash lab.

111
00:06:05,307 --> 00:06:10,342
Two files: lab one thirty dot S H, and shortcuts one thirty dot S H.

112
00:06:10,892 --> 00:06:11,798
Install it,

113
00:06:11,798 --> 00:06:14,682
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

114
00:06:14,682 --> 00:06:16,577
dash m seven five five,

115
00:06:16,577 --> 00:06:18,473
lab one thirty dot S H,

116
00:06:18,473 --> 00:06:19,709
into slash usr,

117
00:06:19,709 --> 00:06:20,698
slash local,

118
00:06:20,698 --> 00:06:21,686
slash S bin.

119
00:06:22,236 --> 00:06:26,885
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one thirty dot S H.

120
00:06:26,885 --> 00:06:28,288
You get three: lab,

121
00:06:28,288 --> 00:06:28,583
P G,

122
00:06:28,583 --> 00:06:29,468
and use D B.

123
00:06:30,018 --> 00:06:31,691
You need post gress Q L,

124
00:06:31,691 --> 00:06:32,946
server and client,

125
00:06:32,946 --> 00:06:33,643
installed,

126
00:06:33,643 --> 00:06:35,874
and port fifty-five oh one free.

127
00:06:35,874 --> 00:06:38,662
The lab runs its own private post gress,

128
00:06:38,662 --> 00:06:39,917
in its own folder.

129
00:06:40,467 --> 00:06:42,174
Lab up prepares everything.

130
00:06:42,174 --> 00:06:43,881
Lab check judges your work.

131
00:06:43,881 --> 00:06:45,398
Lab down removes it all.

132
00:06:45,398 --> 00:06:47,422
Only run it on your own machine.

133
00:06:49,472 --> 00:06:51,541
Pass or fail. You need all five.

134
00:06:52,091 --> 00:06:56,017
One: you measured six orders and fourteen thousand four hundred cents first.

135
00:06:56,567 --> 00:07:00,321
Two: the backup file exists, and P G restore can read it.

136
00:07:00,871 --> 00:07:04,306
Three: the disaster happened, and shop has zero orders.

137
00:07:04,856 --> 00:07:07,629
Four: the new database has all six orders back.

138
00:07:08,179 --> 00:07:11,699
Five: the restored total matches the number you wrote down.

139
00:07:12,249 --> 00:07:13,551
To pass, all five.

140
00:07:15,601 --> 00:07:17,372
Your turn, on your own machine.

141
00:07:17,922 --> 00:07:20,994
One: lab up, use D B, measure, and write the answers.

142
00:07:21,544 --> 00:07:24,082
Two: take the backup, and check it is readable.

143
00:07:24,632 --> 00:07:26,616
Three: run the accidental delete.

144
00:07:27,166 --> 00:07:31,262
Four: create the new database, restore, and validate the numbers.

145
00:07:31,812 --> 00:07:35,631
Five: lab check until you see five out of five. Then lab down.

146
00:07:36,181 --> 00:07:38,016
Pause the video, and begin now.

147
00:07:53,566 --> 00:07:54,441
Quick check.

148
00:07:54,991 --> 00:07:57,337
Why is a backup on the same disk not enough?

149
00:07:57,887 --> 00:08:00,063
Why restore into a new database first?

150
00:08:00,613 --> 00:08:02,576
Which numbers prove a restore worked?

151
00:08:03,126 --> 00:08:05,473
What are recovery point and recovery time?

152
00:08:06,023 --> 00:08:08,427
Objectives covered: M C fourteen,

153
00:08:08,427 --> 00:08:09,447
objective six.

154
00:08:09,447 --> 00:08:11,705
Back up and restore a database,

155
